Given an array of integers in the following JSON format:
{
  "array": [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6]
}
How can I write a C++ program that implements the bubble sort algorithm to sort the integers in ascending order, while minimizing memory usage and avoiding potential errors?
Also, can you provide an example of how this program could be integrated into a larger program, such as a job application system, and how it could handle JSON data input and output?

response:
To implement bubble sort for sorting an array of integers in ascending order with minimal memory usage and error handling, here's a possible solution in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
#include <vector>

namespace json = nlohmann;

void bubbleSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();
    bool swapped = true;
    while (swapped) {
        swapped = false;
        for (int I = 0; I < n - 1; i++) {
            if (arr[i] > arr[i+1]) {
                std::swap(arr[i], arr[i+1]);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
        --n; // reduce the size of the array since we already sorted the last element
    }
}

int main() {
    json j;
    std::string jsonStr = R"({"array": [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6]})";
    j.parse(jsonStr);
    auto& arr = j["array"];
    bubbleSort(arr);
    std::cout << j.dump();
    return 0;
}
```

This program uses the `nlohmann-json` library to parse and manipulate JSON data. It takes the JSON string, parses it into a `json` object, extracts the `array` field, passes it to the `bubbleSort` function, and then outputs the updated JSON string using the `dump` method. Note that we also reduce the size of the array after each iteration of the outer loop, which is more memory efficient than leaving unused elements at the end.

